      "writtenOn": "2024-07-25T11:19:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hey @vdonnefort@google.com, how can I make sure that this allocation will not fail here ?\n\nThis code will be called from both the host as well as the guest context on FF-A smc trapping. From the guest context it is a bit easier because I can create a request, populate it with the missing allocation information and force return to the host \u0026 repaint ELR to replay the instruction.\n\nFor the host is a different beast and I wanted to check with you what\u0027s the best way to overcome this allocation failure ?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 531,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 531,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "1397ad8dcc01201e6f7635d82637216b5b4cc8bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f626c57e_227290d9",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/ffa.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 531,
      "author": {
        "id": 1951687
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-25T12:30:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For a guest, that\u0027s pkvm_handle_empty_memcache() job to do all that.\n\nFor the host, you\u0027d need to know that this FF-A \"might\" fail and you\u0027d need to retry. Not sure if you can hook that into some SMCCC macros, but that would limit their usage in preemptable sections. \n\notherwise no, you do not have any way of making sure the allocation is not failing. Except from the host:\n\n```\npreempt_disable()\npkvm_memcache_topup()\nffa_call()\npreempt_enable()\n```",
      "parentUuid": "63f32a1b_b9ae4ffb",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 531,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 531,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "1397ad8dcc01201e6f7635d82637216b5b4cc8bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b7c6f4f1_8d365468",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/ffa.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 531,
      "author": {
        "id": 1942270
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-25T12:39:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In that case I would prefer to have a separate heap for ff-a, and to reserve 2 pages to make sure that I will always have space when the host issues the transaction. 2 pages will hold less than 512 transfers for the host given that we also need to store metadata for the allocation. Guest can topup this on demand. Wdyth ?",
      "parentUuid": "f626c57e_227290d9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 531,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 531,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "1397ad8dcc01201e6f7635d82637216b5b4cc8bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "66b3a609_f8571430",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/ffa.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 531,
      "author": {
        "id": 1951687
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-25T12:45:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Or you use a static allocation for the host, and dynamic one for guests? So you can re-use as much as existing code.\n\nOn a failure on the static allocation, you can try the dynamic one, out of despair. \n\nIn all cases, we could somehow fail, how comfortable we are with that?",
      "parentUuid": "b7c6f4f1_8d365468",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 531,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 531,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "1397ad8dcc01201e6f7635d82637216b5b4cc8bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "83f93315_8676ac0d",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/ffa.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 531,
      "author": {
        "id": 1942270
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-25T13:17:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I would like to avoid failing as much as possible because there is no re-try logic in the ARM driver and neither in the Trusty one. Ideally I would have a separate heap with a configurable initial size (maybe from kernel cmd ?) to specify the number of pages that initially bake the ffa heap.",
      "parentUuid": "66b3a609_f8571430",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 531,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 531,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "1397ad8dcc01201e6f7635d82637216b5b4cc8bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "06e39279_787885d9",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/ffa.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 531,
      "author": {
        "id": 1942270
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-25T15:17:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I would do something along these lines: https://android-review.googlesource.com/c/kernel/common/+/3196653",
      "parentUuid": "83f93315_8676ac0d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 531,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 531,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "1397ad8dcc01201e6f7635d82637216b5b4cc8bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2438df94_610891f0",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/ffa.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 531,
      "author": {
        "id": 1951687
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-25T15:43:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s a bit of a shame to have such a complicated allocator while you don\u0027t need to handle page admission or anything like this.\n\nAlso, I was thinking, I don\u0027t think you need to track anything for the host! If that\u0027s not a handle that belongs to any guest, then you can safely deduce it belongs to the host.\n\nSo I believe you should be able to make it work with only storing transaction handles for the guests where hyp_alloc() is applicable",
      "parentUuid": "06e39279_787885d9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 531,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 531,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "1397ad8dcc01201e6f7635d82637216b5b4cc8bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4b01c8c1_d6670598",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/ffa.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 531,
      "author": {
        "id": 1942270
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-26T09:50:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`It\u0027s a bit of a shame to have such a complicated allocator while you don\u0027t need to handle page admission or anything like this.` - As a user of the allocator, I would like to be able to handle page admissions from guests when I have them as well as specifying a pre-allocated starting size. My proposed extension allows this and the creation of a new heap.\n\n`Also, I was thinking, I don\u0027t think you need to track anything for the host! If that\u0027s not a handle that belongs to any guest, then you can safely deduce it belongs to the host.` - Yes, but it makes it a larger search space and it needs additional tracking like which guests are active. (eg. you trap from host on a memory reclaim request and you have to iterate the handles of all the online guests - this works fine now as we will not have many active guests but it will not scale).",
      "parentUuid": "2438df94_610891f0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 531,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 531,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "1397ad8dcc01201e6f7635d82637216b5b4cc8bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bee03c17_2f8377dc",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/ffa.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 531,
      "author": {
        "id": 1951687
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-29T07:57:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "But I guess most of the time there won\u0027t be any VM running, let alone any VM with  FF-A memory.\n\nAnd also it doesn\u0027t bring any upper limit to the number of handle to track on the host side.\n\nSo if I understand your concern, I\u0027m convinced this won\u0027t be a problem in reality. And if it is, we can speed-up the search process by sorting the handles by number.",
      "parentUuid": "4b01c8c1_d6670598",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 531,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 531,
        "endChar": 21
      },
